// Every entry is a migration operation that can lose data, allowed on purpose. The rule (docs/upgrade-backup.md, "What an
// upgrade may change"): EXPAND, THEN CONTRACT. A release only adds tables, columns and indexes and stops writing what it
// replaces; a LATER release drops what the release before it no longer reads. Never both in the same release.
//
// An entry needs: the migration id, the provider ("sqlite", "postgres" or "*"), the operation and target exactly as the
// guard prints them, a justification of at least 60 characters saying why no data is lost, and the fixture (a directory of
// tests/upgrade-fixtures, made by a release BEFORE this migration) whose upgrade test proves the data survives. A stale
// entry (nothing it covers) fails the guard just as a missing one does.
//
// Empty today: every shipped migration only adds.
[]
